\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 This is a genuinely solid project design \'97 well-scoped for 5 weeks, methodologically coherent, and the action space choice is well-justified. Here's my honest read on what's strong and what deserves a second look:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 \strokec2 What's strong and shouldn't change
\f0\b0 \
The body-frame waypoint delta action space is the right call \'97 it's the most learnable representation and makes imitation labels natural. The single-policy sequential training (checkpoints instead of separate models) is smart scope management. Keeping vision out of the main loop is the right prioritization. The anti-hack reward design (gate order must dominate progress shaping) shows you've thought through reward misspecification, which many projects miss.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 \strokec2 The biggest gap: you never specify the network architecture
\f0\b0 \
For a DL course this is actually the most important design decision, and the document is silent on it. A few questions you need to answer:\
The state is fundamentally Markovian 
\f2\i if
\f0\i0  you have clean velocity and angular rate readings. But in practice, if those are estimated from noisy position data, you have partial observability \'97 and a feedforward MLP will struggle where a recurrent policy (LSTM or GRU over a short history window) would handle it cleanly. For a DL class, this tradeoff is worth making explicit and justifying either way. The simplest defensible choice is: MLP with history concatenation (last 3\'965 obs stacked) as a baseline, LSTM as an extension. Either way, state it.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 \strokec2 PPO catastrophic forgetting is a real failure mode
\f0\b0 \
This is the most underappreciated risk in BC \uc0\u8594  PPO pipelines. PPO's policy gradient updates can rapidly unlearn the careful gate-following behavior from imitation, especially early in fine-tuning when the reward signal is sparse. Three mitigations to consider:\
One option is a behavior cloning auxiliary loss during PPO (add a small MSE term against the BC policy's actions, decayed over training). Another is a KL penalty keeping the PPO policy close to the DAgger checkpoint. The cleanest option for a DL course framing is probably the auxiliary loss \'97 it's easy to implement, ablate, and explain, and it directly motivates an interesting experiment (loss with vs. without the BC regularizer).\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 \strokec2 Week 1 scope is your highest risk
\f0\b0 \
Gate events + expert (spline generator + tracker that produces body-frame delta labels) + dataset schema + BC baseline is a lot. The expert alone \'97 even the "lazy MPC" version \'97 takes real implementation time if you're building minimum-snap trajectory generation from scratch. Minimum-snap is non-trivial.\
Strong recommendation: use 
\f3\fs26 gym-pybullet-drones
\f0\fs24  (or 
\f3\fs26 Flightmare
\f0\fs24  if you want GPU-accelerated rollouts). These give you a working quadrotor dynamics simulator with an existing interface. Building a custom PyBullet environment from scratch to also have clean gate events is a scope risk in week 1. If you use an existing env, you can focus week 1 on the expert and BC, which is already plenty.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 \strokec2 Action clip relaxation needs a concrete schedule
\f0\b0 \
Starting at 0.25m clip radius is conservative but correct. However, the document says "increase with curriculum" without specifics. At 10Hz with 0.25m clip, max speed is 2.5 m/s \'97 fine for early stages but too slow for racing behavior in PPO. You should decide now: something like 0.25m \uc0\u8594  0.5m \u8594  1.0m tied to curriculum stages 2 \u8594  4 \u8594  6. This matters because if you forget to relax it, PPO fine-tuning for speed will be fighting an artificial ceiling.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 \strokec2 Minor things worth considering
\f0\b0 \
The progress shaping reward 
\f3\fs26 d_prev - d_now
\f0\fs24  (Euclidean distance to next gate) can be gamed by oscillating near the gate. Signed distance along the gate normal is slightly more principled and avoids this.\
Curriculum stages 0 and 1 (hover, waypoint tracking) might be skippable given BC warm-start \'97 the BC policy already knows how to fly by construction. You could start the 
\f2\i RL curriculum
\f0\i0  at stage 2 (single gate) and save the hover/waypoint stages for diagnosing failures if something breaks.\
For a DL class, it's worth having a short section in your writeup on what makes this a deep learning problem specifically \'97 the non-linear function approximation, generalization across gate layouts, the distribution shift problem that motivates DAgger. This will matter for framing.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 \strokec2 Summary
\f0\b0 \
The project is in good shape. The two things I'd prioritize acting on: (1) specify and justify the network architecture now, and (2) add a BC auxiliary loss during PPO to guard against forgetting. Both are also good DL-specific contributions that strengthen the course framing. The rest are risk mitigations, not blockers.\
}